Why. `spawn objective retire` (objective 210) takes a free-text reason, and nothing checks a claim that the work is on main. At 15:04 on 2026-11-18 the supervisor retired 9/61 "already on main" on the strength of the owner's contract landing title (87315e67), while 061.sh was red on main: 061's src had not landed. No un-retire exists, so the objective was closed with its work undone. A record the gate already writes says what the title cannot:
- Every gate run lists, in its verdict's `passed`, the regressions it ran on the candidate and saw pass, and in `reused` those whose pass it took from the base (objective 056). The integrator copies both onto the `task.gated` record.
- An accepted task.gated of a task that then landed is about a candidate that became main.
- A later gate that finds main red on a scenario names it in `main_red_scenarios`.
9/61 has no accepted, landed gate of mission 9 listing 061.sh as passed. A legitimately renumbered objective (061 -> 063) has one: 063's landed gate lists 061.sh.
The lead's call: --kind is required, not guessed from the reason's words. A text guard on the words is the kind of inference this contract exists to remove.

What changes:
1. `spawn objective retire --mission <m> --objective <n> --kind on-main|renumbered|obsolete --by <name> --reason <text>`.
   - --kind is required ("--kind is required" and the usage, as for the other flags).
   - The record gains `kind`: {type objective.retired, id, objective, kind, by, reason, caller}.
   - spawn_objective_retire's input has exactly type, id, objective, kind, by and reason. A kind other than the three strings is refused with "a retirement record's kind is on-main, renumbered or obsolete, not <kind>".
2. kind on-main is recorded only when the store holds an accepted `task.gated` of a task of the same mission, that task now `landed`, whose `passed` or `reused` names the objective's scenario.
   - The scenario's name is `<NNN>.sh`, the number zero-padded to at least three digits, as objectives.rs names it (format "{:03}.sh"): objective 9 is 009.sh, objective 1234 is 1234.sh.
   - It must also hold that no task.gated of the mission recorded after the newest such gate names the scenario in `main_red_scenarios`.
   - The check runs after every existing refusal (in flight, closed), so those keep their texts.
3. A refused on-main records nothing and says, without a red since:
   "objective <m>/<n> is not shown on main: no accepted gate of a landed task of mission <m> lists <NNN>.sh as passed (a landing's title is not a gate record); run its scenario through a gate, or retire it as renumbered or obsolete"
   With a red since, naming the first red gate after the pass, its task and its record's base:
   "objective <m>/<n> is not shown on main: <NNN>.sh is red on main since task #<t>'s gate (<base>); run its scenario through a gate, or retire it as renumbered or obsolete"
4. renumbered and obsolete need no record.
5. The callers say the kind:
   - main.rs's usage line;
   - supervise.rs's tool line, which also says that on-main needs the gate's record (an accepted gate of a landed task of the mission that ran its scenario and saw it pass, with no gate since finding it red on main) and that a landing's title is not one;
   - rsi/scenarios/210.sh (owner): its retires pass --kind (renumbered for 3 and 6, obsolete otherwise), and its expected records name the kind.

Cost (R2, the lead's timing of R1 on live: the whole read took 1.5-1.8 s, and under the append lock every record would wait behind it; the bar is under 50 ms under the lock).
- Before the lock, spawn_objective_retire reads the mission's task.gated records that may show it: accepted ones naming the scenario passed or reused, and ones naming it red on main. It reads them up to the newest control event when it began, v_seen. Every append takes the lock and numbers the event max + 1, so every event up to v_seen is committed.
- Under the lock it reads only the control events after v_seen (a range of spawn_events_session_seq), and the states of the tasks read: a landing since then counts.
- It runs only on a retire by hand with kind on-main (no polled path reads it); renumbered and obsolete skip it.
- The package carries the read-only SELECTs of both parts for the lead to time on live.

Migration (crates/spawn-postgres/sql/schema.sql, applied whole at every start, through the normal deploy): `create or replace function spawn_objective_retire` only. No table, column, index or grant changes. Existing objective.retired records (no kind) stay as they are and fold as before.

What stays as it is:
- who may retire (the owner's and the supervisor's logins) and how caller is set;
- by and reason and their limits;
- every refusal 210 lists, with its text;
- how a retired objective reads: the fold, the board's and the RSI page's `retired`, and OBJECTIVE_LANDINGS. The kind is in the record only; the board does not show it.
- the gate and the integrator: no new record, no new field.

